---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/use-cases/post-purchase';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/post-purchase');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/post-purchase');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/use-cases" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Plan Shopify post-purchase education</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">Help a buyer use the product and understand delivery before asking for another purchase.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
      <h2>Use the right milestone</h2>
      <p>Order acceptance, payment, fulfillment and delivery answer different questions. Build separate messages for those states and assign an owner to operational notices so a marketing flow does not duplicate the receipt. If delivery is unavailable, use cautious copy about the order status rather than claiming the parcel arrived.</p>
      <h2>Match education to the purchased item</h2>
      <p>A size guide may help before dispatch, while assembly or care instructions belong where they are useful during use. For a split shipment, show which items the instructions concern. Digital access needs an access destination and support route rather than a parcel-tracking block. Keep gift recipient details out of purchaser marketing copy.</p>
      <h2>Handle exceptions before cross-sell</h2>
      <p>A cancellation should stop product onboarding; a return may need a different support path. An open delivery problem should pause promotional asks without removing essential order updates. Active subscriptions need renewal and usage context, not an invitation to buy the same subscription again.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate event and content ownership</h2>
      <p>Ask candidates to demonstrate line-item mapping, split shipments, late delivery updates and cancellation handling. Decide which team maintains instructions when a product changes. A lifecycle editor is useful only if its data and destinations support those tasks; no ranked app winner or universal delay follows from this use case.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise</h2>
      <p>Create a mixed order, fulfill one line, then cancel the other. Inspect the resulting education and links on a staff profile before enabling the flow. Measure support topics, returns and repeat contribution over a declared observation window; a click on instructions is evidence of engagement, not proof that anxiety fell.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
